  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>)</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math>:</p><p>Clearly, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>)</mo></math> term represents, electromagnetic wave is travelling along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></math> direction or along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math>.</p><p>Now, in vector form, direction of electromagnetive wave is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>×</mo><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math>. Therefore, magnetic field is along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math>. </p><p>Also, we know that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>C</mi></mfrac></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> is speed of light.</p><p>Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mi>cos</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>)</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math>.<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math></p>"
  }
